Descriptions.Recommendations:
By default, Azure SQL databases are encrypted using Microsoft service-managed keys. Consider using a customer-managed key to better protect the encrypted data.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It defines an Azure SQL database that uses customer-managed keys.
<pre>
resource "azurerm_mssql_server" "aztf010-tn-01" {
  name                         = "aztf010-tn-01"
  ...
}
resource "azurerm_mssql_server_transparent_data_encryption" "test" {
  server_id        = azurerm_mssql_server.aztf010-tn-01.id
  key_vault_key_id = azurerm_key_vault_key.example.id
}</pre>

Consult organization policies and, if appropriate, update the configuration to use customer-managed keys.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example improves the configuration shown in <code>Example 1</code>. It defines an Azure disk snapshot with an <code>encryption_settings</code> block that contains disk and key encryption keys.
<pre>
resource "azurerm_snapshot" "aztf012-tn-01" {
  name                = "snapshot"
  ...
  create_option       = "Copy"
  ...
  encryption_settings {
    disk_encryption_key {
      secret_url      = azurerm_key_vault_secret.example.id
      source_vault_id = azurerm_key_vault.example.id
    }
    key_encryption_key {
      key_url         = azurerm_key_vault_key.example.id
      source_vault_id = azurerm_key_vault.example.id
    }
  }
}
</pre>

Consider adding a customer-managed key to better protect the shared image contents.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example improves the configuration in <code>Example 1</code>. It defines a shared image with a <code>target_region</code> block that specifies a disk encryption set.
<pre>
resource "azurerm_shared_image_version" "aztf016-02-01" {
  name = "0.0.1"
  ...
  target_region {
    name = azurerm_resource_group.example.location
    regional_replica_count = 1
    disk_encryption_set_id = azurerm_disk_encryption_set.example.id
  }
}</pre>

A BigQuery dataset's CMEK is the default key for its constituent table when CMEK is unspecified. This prevents BigQuery tables from missing CMEK.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. The <code>kms_key_name</code> argument in the <code>default_encryption_configuration</code> block specifies a CMEK stored in the Google Cloud KMS to protect DEK.
<pre>
resource "google_bigquery_dataset" "dataset_instance" {
...
  default_encryption_configuration {
    kms_key_name = "projects/KMS_PROJECT_ID/locations/LOCATION/keyRings/KEY_RING/cryptoKeys/KEY"
  }
...
}
</pre>
